# Line endings — the first thing Windows breaks (NFR-018).
#
# Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true, and actions/checkout does
# not override it. Without this file, a windows-latest runner checks out
# every text file with CRLF: the go:embed corpus (help pages, templates,
# translations) arrives two bytes longer per line, every golden comparison
# in the suite fails on invisible characters, and `gofmt -l` reports the
# whole tree. None of that is a Windows defect in the product — it is the
# checkout lying about the bytes the repository holds.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalizes to LF in the repository AND in the working
# tree, on every platform. On Linux and macOS it is a no-op (autocrlf is
# false or input there); on Windows it is what makes the checkout match
# what the tests were written against.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Binary fixtures — never touched, never diffed as text.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.pdf binary
*.tar binary
*.tgz binary
*.gz binary
*.zip binary
